    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Originalism advocates for interpreting the Second Amendment based on the public understanding at the time of its ratification, emphasizing historical context and understood limitations. Conversely, living constitutionalists argue the Amendment's meaning must evolve with societal changes, allowing interpretations to shift based on modern needs and technological advancements. This creates tension over whether the Founders intended an absolute right or a rights framework adaptable to contemporary life. Essentially, the debate lies between anchoring the text to the past versus allowing it to speak to the present.",
